WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.036
- [Automated Alarm Clock] Wake up.

00:00:01.036 --> 00:00:02.594
- [Narrator] Before this, and this,

00:00:02.594 --> 00:00:04.177
and this, and this.

00:00:05.858 --> 00:00:07.578
Ahhh, that sound.

00:00:07.578 --> 00:00:10.512
People in Britain and
Ireland woke up like this.

00:00:10.512 --> 00:00:13.719
That's right, being an
alarm clock used to be a job

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:16.719
and they were called Knocker Uppers.

00:00:21.352 --> 00:00:23.275
We're traveling back in time for this one,

00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:24.910
so let's get a historian in here.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:25.989
- Do I sit here, or...?

00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:28.541
- [Producer] Yup
- Yeah, fantastic.

00:00:28.541 --> 00:00:30.791
Oh, sorry I'm late the
Knocker Upper didn't call.

00:00:30.791 --> 00:00:32.092
- [Narrator] Good one, Richard.

00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:33.635
- My name is Richard
Jones, and I'm an author

00:00:33.635 --> 00:00:35.206
on the history of London.

00:00:35.206 --> 00:00:38.203
- [Narrotor] OK, let's
see if we got this right.

00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:41.703
From the early 1800s to
the 1960s, waking people up

00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:43.886
was a paid job?
- [Richard Jones] Yes.

00:00:43.886 --> 00:00:45.727
- [Narrator] And this was
done with a bamboo pole?

00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:46.811
(tapping at the window)

00:00:46.811 --> 00:00:48.222
- Oh yes, most certainly.

00:00:48.222 --> 00:00:50.580
This is the age where people
started to work in factories.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:51.713
(Factory whistle blowing)

00:00:51.713 --> 00:00:53.930
They’ve got to be up early for their shift.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.560
And, of course, most people
are on minimal wages.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.352
They haven't got alarm
clocks in their house,

00:00:58.352 --> 00:01:00.197
consequently, there's no one wake them up.

00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:01.670
So that's where the knocker up,

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.112
or the Knocker Upper came in.

00:01:03.112 --> 00:01:04.932
- [Narrator] See, in big industrial cities

00:01:04.932 --> 00:01:07.229
like London, Birmingham and Manchester,

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:10.625
Knocker Uppers used poles
to reach the windows.

00:01:10.625 --> 00:01:12.955
And these poles had a
hook or knob at the end,

00:01:12.955 --> 00:01:14.587
which made them pretty effective.

00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:16.430
- You could scratch that handle,

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.839
a bit like nails going down a blackboard.

00:01:18.839 --> 00:01:20.837
- [Chorus] Wakey-wakey.

00:01:20.837 --> 00:01:22.571
- [Narrator] Other Knocker
Uppers, mostly women,

00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:25.008
used peashooters to aim at the windows.

00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:26.666
(pea flying through the air)

00:01:26.666 --> 00:01:27.499
- Oigh!

00:01:27.499 --> 00:01:28.651
- [Narrator] (laughs) Or not.

00:01:28.651 --> 00:01:29.771
But why not just shout?

00:01:29.771 --> 00:01:31.378
- Wake up! Wake up!
- Shhh.

00:01:31.378 --> 00:01:32.211
- Uggh.
- [Narrator] Well,

00:01:32.211 --> 00:01:34.093
you do have to keep the neighbors in mind.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:37.146
So the big question is who
knocks up the Knocker Upper?

00:01:37.146 --> 00:01:38.119
(knocking at the door)

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:39.454
- The evidence suggests that

00:01:39.454 --> 00:01:40.737
the Knocker Upper just didn't go to bed.

00:01:40.737 --> 00:01:42.796
Sometimes the Knocker
Upper might attune himself

00:01:42.796 --> 00:01:45.060
to be able to wake up a three a.m.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.230
- [Narrator] Aggh, mystery solved.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:48.566
- [Chorus] Yay!

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:50.472
- [Narrator] Play us out, Richard.

00:01:50.472 --> 00:01:53.621
- We had a knocker up and our
knocker up had a knocker up.

00:01:53.621 --> 00:01:57.377
And our knocker up's knocker
up didn't knock our knocker up.

00:01:57.377 --> 00:01:59.404
So our knocker up didn't knock us up.

00:01:59.404 --> 00:02:00.904
'Cause he's not up.

